{0 Systems}

A system is a unit of logic built with {!Eon_ecs.System.Default.make}. It
holds up to five optional callbacks — [register], [update], [on_signal],
[on_event], [on_command] — and nothing is mandatory: a pure logic system
omits the bus handlers, a handler-only system omits [update]. Examples
below are from the companion
{{:https://github.com/imunitic/eon/blob/main/eon_ecs/examples/book/systems_example.ml}[systems_example.ml]}.

{1 An update-only system}

The common case — read/write the world every tick, no bus involvement:

{[
  module System = Eon_ecs.System.Default

  let movement_system () =
    System.make
      ~update:(fun world dt ->
        Query.iter2 world Position.name Velocity.name
          (fun entity (pos : Position.t) (vel : Velocity.t) ->
             let moved =
               { Position.x = pos.x +. (vel.dx *. dt); y = pos.y +. (vel.dy *. dt) }
             in
             World.set_component world entity ~name:Position.name moved))
      ~kind:`Variable
      ()
]}

[kind] defaults to [`Variable] if omitted — it's set explicitly here only
for clarity. See {{!page-progress_and_loop}Progress and Loop} for what
[`Fixed] changes about when [update] gets called.

{1 A handler-only system}

Omit [update] entirely for a system that only reacts to bus messages.
[register] runs once, before the pipeline's first tick — the natural place
to seed any world-level state this system's handlers depend on:

{[
  let spawn_system () =
    let spawned = ref 0 in
    System.make
      ~register:(fun _world -> spawned := 0)
      ~on_command:(fun world (`Spawn_at (x, y)) ->
        let e = World.create_entity world in
        World.add_component world e ~name:Position.name { Position.x = x; y };
        incr spawned)
      ()
]}

{1 Driving a system directly}

A {!Eon_ecs.Pipeline} (next chapter) automates dispatch across many
systems, but a single system's callbacks can also be driven directly —
useful for understanding exactly what the pipeline automates, and
occasionally useful standalone (a focused test, a one-off script):

{[
  let world  = make_world () in
  let system = movement_system () in

  let e = World.create_entity world in
  World.add_component world e ~name:Position.name { Position.x = 0.0; y = 0.0 };
  World.add_component world e ~name:Velocity.name { Velocity.dx = 2.0; dy = 0.0 };

  System.run system world 1.0;   (* one manual "tick" at dt = 1.0 *)

  match World.get_component world e ~name:Position.name with
  | Some (p : Position.t) -> assert (p.x = 2.0)
  | None -> assert false
]}

{!Eon_ecs.System.Default.run} calls the [update] callback, if present, with
the given [world] and [dt] — nothing more. A system with no [update] simply
does nothing on [run].

{1 Wiring bus handlers by hand: register, then attach}

A reactive system's handlers don't run on their own — they have to be
subscribed to real bus instances. {!Eon_ecs.Pipeline.S.register_all}
performs exactly these two steps automatically for every system in a
pipeline; seeing them called directly makes clear what that automation is
actually doing:

{[
  let world    = World.create () in
  Position.register world;
  let system   = spawn_system () in
  let signals  = Signals.create () in
  let events   = Events.create () in
  let commands = Commands.create () in

  System.register system world;                             (* runs ~register *)
  System.attach   system world ~signals ~events ~commands;   (* subscribes handlers *)

  Commands.emit commands (`Spawn_at (3.0, 4.0));
  Commands.drain commands;   (* on_command fires here *)

  assert (World.count_entities world = 1)
]}

[register] and [attach] are separate calls for a reason: [register] is
"world setup this system needs," [attach] is "start listening" — a
pipeline always does both together via [register_all], but the split
matters for systems built outside a pipeline entirely.

